 *
 * A second read of the same commit, and not a second spelling of the check-run one:
 * GitHub creates a status context when the job producing it starts, so between a run
 * starting and its first job registering there is a run and no context. `check-runs`
 * cannot answer that — the absence it reports is exactly the one this questions.
 *
 * Degrades to an empty list on failure rather than failing the call. What empty means
 * here is "no run is known to be going", which is the answer this module gave before
 * the read existed: a `checks-missing` blocker and one dispatch attempt. So a failure
 * costs a duplicate run at worst, where throwing would refuse a merge over a read that
 * only refines a blocker.
